{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "What Is a Requirement Analysis?"}, {"type": "position", "content": 1}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "A2.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when describing requirement analysis."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe common activities at work using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Interpret the requirements report to determine the technological needs in managing information, based on rules and protocols established by the company."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Participates in simple conversations or simulated situations about the communicative topics for this level through grammar and vocabulary structures.  Recognizes simple ideas on requirement reports, tools for Object Oriented Programming, and other basic programming tools."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob01re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The Present Simple and the Present Continuous - Affirmative Form"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We use both <span class=\"strong\">present simple </span> and <span class=\"strong\">present continuous </span> to talk about activities performed in the present; still the structures are very different.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Simple present </span> is a structure used to talk about permanent situations in the present and general facts that are not likely to change. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Examples: <br> <span class=\"italic\"> \u2022\tWe <span class=\"strong\">work </span> at a big multinational. <br> \u2022\tI <span class=\"strong\">know </span> about information systems.  </span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "The <span class=\"strong\">simple present </span> has a very simple structure in the <span class=\"strong\">affirmative </span>; there is only one important fact you need to remember: when you talk indirectly about one single person or object you add an \u201c<span class=\"strong\">s </span>\u201d or an \u201c<span class=\"strong\">es </span>\u201d to the action. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Examples: <br> <span class=\"italic\"> \u2022\tI work with computers. <br> \u2022\tYou work at the same company. <br> \u2022\t<span class=\"strong\">He knows </span> a lot about computers.  </span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Pronouns that usually have the <span class=\"strong\"> -s </span> or <span class=\"strong\"> -es </span> in the simple present are <span class=\"strong\">he, she </span> and <span class=\"strong\">it </span>. These are known as the <span class=\"strong\">third person singular </span>.<br>   There are some rules to add the <span class=\"strong\"> -s </span> to the third person singular: "}, {"type": "text", "content": "1.\tIf the verb finishes with the vowel <span class=\"strong\"> -o we add -es </span> to the verb.<br> 2.\tIf the verb finishes with a <span class=\"strong\">consonant </span>plus -y </span> we change <span class=\"strong\"> <span class=\"strong\"> -y </span> for <span class=\"strong\"> -i </span> and add -es </span>.<br> 3.\tIf the verb finishes with a <span class=\"strong\">vowel </span> plus <span class=\"strong\"> -y</span>. we just add the <span class=\"strong\"> -s</span>. to the verb. <br> 4.\tIf the verb finishes with <span class=\"strong\">  -s, -ss, -ch, -sh, -x </span>, or any other sound similar to the <span class=\"strong\"> -ch </span>, we add <span class=\"strong\"> -es </span> to the verb. <br> 5.\tFor the verb <span class=\"strong\"> have</span>, change it for <span class=\"strong\"> has</span> in the third person. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Examples:<br> <span class=\"italic\"> \u2022\tGo-go <span class=\"strong\">es</span><br> \u2022\tApply-appli <span class=\"strong\">es</span><br> \u2022\tEnjoy-enjoy <span class=\"strong\">s</span><br> \u2022\tWatch- watch <span class=\"strong\">es</span><br> \u2022\tHave- <span class=\"strong\">has</span><br> </span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "We use <span class=\"strong\">Present continuous</span> to talk about temporary facts in the present, things that are happening just at the moment, but are going to change in the future. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Examples: <br> <span class=\"italic\"> \u2022\tThis year <span class=\"strong\">we're</span> living in Bogot\u00e1. <br> \u2022\tI'm <span class=\"strong\">studying</span> systems at the moment. <br> </span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Notice that <span class=\"strong\">present continuous</span> is usually accompanied by temporary time expressions. <br> Do not forget the verb <span class=\"strong\">to be</span> when you are making a <span class=\"strong\">present continuous </span>sentence. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Examples:<br> <span class=\"italic\"> \u2022\tNow I <span class=\"strong\">am working</span> at a systems company.   <br> \u2022\tWe <span class=\"strong\">are repairing</span> systems at a caf\u00e9 at the moment. <br> </span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "There are some verbs in English we don't use in the present continuous, even if we are talking about a temporary situation happening at the moment of speaking. Those verbs are the ones related to emotions (like, hate) and verbs connected to mental functions (think, remember)."}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":4,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div class=\\\"center_text\\\"><strong>Simple present (permanent) </strong></div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div class=\\\"center_text\\\"><strong>Present continuous (temporary) </strong></div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">I repair computers. </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">I'm repairing computers at the moment.</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">We know about systems. </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">I know what I'm doing! </span>\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" instruction_table_background intro_table_border\"}"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Imagine we are working on a very important project."}, {"type": "example", "content": "We are doing the requirement analysis, which is always the starting point of any project."}, {"type": "example", "content": "As we are doing the requirement analysis of a project, we need to know step by step how it works."}, {"type": "example", "content": "The project we are working on is very complex, but we have to be analytic, and assertive to make the project work."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Common Collocations Related to Computers"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "Collocation are pair of words or more words that usually go together in a sentence. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "There are some verbs that are usually accompanied by the same complements when we talk about computers.<br> Those collocations are very common in the computing jargon, and refer to actions we perform all the time when working with computers. <br> On the graph bellow there are some of the most common collocations you need to learn. <br>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Participants have to deal with thousands of requirements, examine and record data using several techniques, accessing to files and downloading relevant information."}, {"type": "image", "content": "enpe8n3le01ob01img01.jpg"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "requirement"}, {"type": "definition", 